<header><h1>Gravity Rift: Proof</h1></header>
<article>
<p>The source text behind each note on <a href="/R_gravity_rift">Gravity Rift</a>. <b>Explicit</b> sources state the note directly. <b>Implicit</b> sources support it without stating it.</p>

<h2>Rules Reference</h2>
    <ul>
    <li>(Thunder&rsquo;s Edge) A gravity rift can affect the same ship multiple times during a single movement, but it contributes its movement bonus to that ship only once.<sup>6,7</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>7</sup> Thunder&rsquo;s Edge Rulebook p. 16, Clarifications: &ldquo;A gravity rift can only contribute its movement bonus to ships that pass through it once per ship. This is a change from previous rulings.&rdquo;</li>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus, &ldquo;If a player moves a ship through the same gravity rift multiple times in one action, do they still have to roll the dice multiple times?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes, although the movement bonus is once per ship per tactical action, the negative roll requirement remains per time a ship moves out of or through a gravity rift.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    </ul>

<h2>Notes</h2>
    <ol class="note">
    <li>Moving into a gravity rift, usually when it is the active system, will not provide the +1, nor will the ships have to roll for removal.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How do gravity rifts work: when do I get +1 move and when do I roll?&rdquo;: &ldquo;A ship that stops in a rift gains nothing and doesn&rsquo;t roll until it later leaves.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If a ship moves through or out of multiple gravity rifts, each gravity rift provides +1 to its move value and requires a separate roll for removal. If it moves out of the same gravity rift more than once, that gravity rift provides +1 only once, but each instance requires a separate roll.<sup>1,2,6,7,8</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How do gravity rifts work: when do I get +1 move and when do I roll?&rdquo;: &ldquo;gets +1 move for that path, one per rift &hellip; Each ship that leaves or passes through a rift rolls.&rdquo;</li>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>1</sup> LRR quick reference: &ldquo;Each Ship Moving Through or Out of Applies +1 Move and Rolls 1 die.&rdquo;</li>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>8</sup> PoK rulebook quick reference: the same text.</li>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus, &ldquo;If a player moves a ship through the same gravity rift multiple times in one action, do they still have to roll the dice multiple times?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes, although the movement bonus is once per ship per tactical action, the negative roll requirement remains per time a ship moves out of or through a gravity rift.&rdquo;</li>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>7</sup> Thunder&rsquo;s Edge Rulebook, Clarifications: &ldquo;A gravity rift can only contribute its movement bonus to ships that pass through it once per ship. This is a change from previous rulings.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A ship removed by a gravity rift is not destroyed.<sup>2,10</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>10</sup> Codex I: &ldquo;units that fail to survive the roll are removed from the board and returned to players&rsquo; reinforcements rather than being destroyed.&rdquo;</li>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When a carrier leaves a gravity rift &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Units lost this way are removed rather than destroyed.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>All ship movement must be declared before any of them roll for gravity rift removal. A player may not choose additional ships to move after seeing the result of this roll.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Do ships move one at a time, and can I wait to see gravity rift rolls before moving others?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; you declare every moving ship and they move together; rift rolls come after.&rdquo;</li>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>1</sup> LRR 41.2: &ldquo;one die is rolled immediately before it exits the gravity rift system.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Each ship rolls for gravity rift removal as it exits the gravity rift, during movement. The fleet pool limit in the active system is checked only after all moving ships arrive, so ships removed by a gravity rift do not count against it.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>1</sup> LRR 41.2: &ldquo;one die is rolled immediately before it exits the gravity rift system.&rdquo;</li>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Do ships move one at a time, and can I wait to see gravity rift rolls before moving others?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Fleet pool isn&rsquo;t checked in systems you pass through, only at the end, and you remove any excess then.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Some effects allow ships to move out of or through a gravity rift without rolling for removal, such as the <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/Relics#Codex_IV:~:text=Circlet%20of%20the%20Void"><i>Circlet of the Void</i></a> relic and the <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/Action_Cards#Codex_I:~:text=Nav%20Suite"><i>Nav Suite</i></a> action card.<sup>11</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>11</sup> Codex IV, Circlet of the Void: &ldquo;Your units do not roll for gravity rifts, and you ignore the movement effects of other anomalies.&rdquo;</li>
        <li><b>Unverified</b>: Nav Suite: see the C_action_cards note &ldquo;A player may move ships through or out of a gravity rift without rolling for removal.&rdquo; (*)</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If a ship with capacity is removed by a gravity rift, any units it would have picked up later in its movement, after exiting the gravity rift, remain where they are.<sup>1</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>1</sup> LRR 95.1b: &ldquo;Units being transported by a ship that is removed from the board by a gravity rift are also removed from the board.&rdquo; Units not yet picked up are not being transported.</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Retreating from a gravity rift will result in the following:</li>
    <ol>
        <li>The retreating player must declare which, if any, fighters and ground forces they are moving, and which ships will be transporting them. If they have the Fighter II technology, then for each fighter, they must choose if that fighter is moving itself or being transported.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When a carrier leaves a gravity rift, do the fighters and ground forces it carries roll too?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Declare which ship carries what before rolling.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
        <li>Each non&ndash;fighter ship and each Fighter II moving itself will have to roll for removal. Transported units are also removed if the ship transporting them is removed.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>1</sup> LRR 41.2: &ldquo;For each ship that would move out of or through a gravity rift, one die is rolled immediately before it exits the gravity rift system&rdquo;; 41.2b: &ldquo;Units that are being transported are removed from the board if the ship transporting them is removed from the board.&rdquo; A retreat moves ships out of the system.</li>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When a carrier leaves a gravity rift, do the fighters and ground forces it carries roll too?&rdquo;: &ldquo;only the ships moving out roll &hellip; if a carrier is lost, everything it carries goes with it.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
        <li>The +1 will have no effect, as retreats are restricted to adjacent systems.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Where can my ships retreat to?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Movement bonuses don&rsquo;t matter, as a retreat always goes to an adjacent system.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
        <li>If all ships are lost to the rift, the retreating player will not place a command token in the destination system.<sup>1</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>1</sup> LRR, Space Combat, Retreat step: &ldquo;If any of a player&rsquo;s units successfully retreat and are moved into an adjacent system, that player must place a command token from their reinforcements in the system to which their units retreated.&rdquo; No unit arrives if every ship is removed.</li>
        </ul></li>
    </ol>
    <li>A system containing a <a href="/F_vuilraith">Vuil&rsquo;raith</a> <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/The_Vuil&#x27;Raith_Cabal#:~:text=Dimensional%20Tear">Dimensional Tear</a> is an anomaly.<sup>2,4</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>4</sup> Designer ruling: &ldquo;Are systems that contain Vuil&rsquo;raith Cabal&rsquo;s Dimensional Tears anomalies? A: Yes.&rdquo;</li>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Do Vuil&rsquo;raith ships roll for Cormund&rsquo;s gravity rift &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;it is simply a gravity rift, and counts as an anomaly for objectives.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The movement restrictions of anomalies are intended to apply to all units, not only ships.<sup>4</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>4</sup> Designer ruling: &ldquo;Any units moving into, through or out of anomalies must follow the same rules for ships moving into, through or out of anomalies. This includes units being moved by Mahact&rsquo;s Benediction or moving ground forces with G&rsquo;hom Sek&rsquo;kus.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    </ol>
</article>
